! >>> THIS DECK IS NOT IN THE SUITE YET, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. <<<
!
! The counters it tests are NOT IMPLEMENTED.  The deck is committed
! ahead of them because every expected value in it is derived from
! the manual rather than from a run, so it is a specification that
! happens to be executable -- and because the implementation attempt
! that produced it failed for a reason worth recording rather than
! rediscovering.  docs/NEXT.md has the whole account; the short
! version is that word 7 acquires a SECOND writer in a rule that
! already has one, and bsc cannot order them through writeWord's
! address dispatch.  Three successive fixes each moved the conflict
! rather than removing it, ending in a combinational cycle through
! the look-ahead.  The real fix is to thread the write out of
! execute() the way the indicator register already is, which is a
! scheduling refactor and not a one-arm change.
!
! Wire it into tests/run_tests.sh when it passes:
!   run "..." ./run_program.sh ../decks/connective_counts.strap StretchConnCntTb \
!       "PC=2086" "cnt_punch_at_left:1079=0x2000" \
!       "cnt_punch_two_in:1080=0x20000002000" \
!       "cnt_punch_at_right:1081=0x60000002000" \
!       "cnt_no_punch:1082=0x70000000000" \
!       "cnt_four_punches:1083=0x8000" \
!       "cnt_cm_counts_contracted:1084=0x9000000c000"
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!
! THE CONNECTIVE COUNTS -- the left-zeros and all-ones counters at
! word 7.  Reference Manual printed p.75, "Counts and Indicators":
!
!   "Two counts describing the result field are available after a
!   connective operation.  The seven-bit all-ones counter, bits 44-50
!   of location 7, contains a count of all ones in the result field.
!   The seven-bit left-zeros counter, bits 17-23 of location 7,
!   contains a count of [p.76:] the number of zeros in the result
!   field that are to the left of the most significant one-bit."
!
! and p.76 on which field they are counted over:
!
!   "Both counters operate modulo 128.  Each count is on the final
!   result field.  IN CONNECT AND CONNECT FOR TEST, THE COUNTS ARE
!   MADE ON THE RESULT FIELD WITH BYTE SIZE EIGHT.  IN CONNECT TO
!   MEMORY, THE COUNTS ARE MADE ON THE RESULT FIELD AFTER IT IS
!   CONVERTED TO THE BYTE SIZE OF THE STORAGE OPERAND."
!
! ARRIVED VIA MCP, and the reason is worth stating because it is not
! obvious from the counters' names.  Printed p.17 lines 32-36 convert
! an IOCD card's channel number from Hollerith:
!
!     X132    CT0011(V+I)(BU,7), .12($4)   -TEST TENS POSITION OF
!                                           CHANNEL
!             LCV(DU,8,4), $LZC+.3, 46     -CONVERT TO OCTAL NUMBER
!     X132AA  CT0011(V+I)(BU,16), .22($4)  -TEST UNITS POSITION
!             +(BU,4), $LZC+.3, 46         -FORM CHANNEL NUMBER
!
! A HOLLERITH DIGIT IS ONE PUNCH AMONG SEVERAL ROWS, so CONNECT FOR
! TEST over the column leaves THE DIGIT ITSELF as the left-zeros
! count -- and the two reads of "$LZC+.3" are how MCP gets the tens
! and units digits out.  Without these counters that sequence
! converts nothing, which is why building LCV alone did not make
! printed p.17 run.
!
! THE COUNTERS ARE DESIGNED TO BE USED AS INDEX VALUES, which p.76
! says outright: "The position of the left-zeros counter in the first
! half word of location 7 is such that it corresponds to that part of
! an index value field which indexes the bit address of an
! instruction.  The position of the all-ones counter in the second
! half-word of location 7 is such that it facilitates indexing of
! half-word addresses."
!
! HOW EACH BLOCK REPORTS.  The connective runs, then word 7 is loaded
! whole and stored, so each check is the entire counter word: the
! left-zeros count at IBM bits 17-23 and the all-ones count at 44-50,
! with everything else required to be zero.  Storing the word rather
! than one field is what makes a count landing in the wrong place a
! failure instead of an invisible success.
!
!   T1  one punch at the left of a seven-bit field: 0 zeros to its
!       left, 1 one-bit.
!   T2  the punch two positions in: 2 and 1.  This is the shape
!       MCP's Hollerith digits take.
!   T3  the punch at the right end: 6 and 1.
!   T4  NO PUNCH AT ALL: 7 and 0.  **The only part of this the page
!       does not state** -- with no one-bit there is nothing for the
!       zeros to be to the left of, so the count is the field width.
!       Labelled as the inference it is, here and in StretchALU.
!   T5  four punches: 0 zeros to the left of the leftmost, and an
!       all-ones count of 4, which is the only block where the two
!       counters disagree about how many bits matter.
!   T6  CONNECT TO MEMORY, where the count is taken AFTER contraction
!       to the storage byte size.  3476 in a 21-bit field is 9 zeros
!       to the left of its top bit and 6 one-bits.  Counting the
!       byte-expanded form instead -- 56 bits with the same six ones
!       spread over seven bytes -- would give 44 and 6.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

! -- 1-5: CONNECT FOR TEST over a seven-bit column ---------------
START   CT0011(BU,7), S1+.57, 0
        L(BU,64,8), 7.0, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T1, 0

        CT0011(BU,7), S2+.57, 0
        L(BU,64,8), 7.0, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T2, 0

        CT0011(BU,7), S3+.57, 0
        L(BU,64,8), 7.0, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T3, 0

        CT0011(BU,7), S4+.57, 0
        L(BU,64,8), 7.0, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T4, 0

        CT0011(BU,7), S5+.57, 0
        L(BU,64,8), 7.0, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T5, 0

! -- 6: CONNECT TO MEMORY counts the CONTRACTED field ------------
        L(BU,64,8), WIDE, 0
        CM0101(BU,21,3), DEST+.43, 0
        L(BU,64,8), 7.0, 0
        ST(BU,64,8), T6, 0

PASS    BEW, $

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this the 64-bit
! fields below would straddle two words.
        SLC, (8)2060.0

S1      DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000100
S2      DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000020
S3      DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000001
S4      DD(BU,64,8), 0
S5      DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000125
! 3476 already in seven eight-bit bytes, the form CM contracts back.
WIDE    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000601401004
DEST    DD(BU,64,8), 0

T1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T2      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T3      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T4      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T5      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T6      DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
